<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<section xml:id="chapter5_structures">
  <title>Structures</title>

  <introduction>
    <p>
      So far, variables have usually represented individual pieces of data.
      Sometimes several values naturally belong together. For example, a point
      on a coordinate plane has both an <c>x</c>-coordinate and a
      <c>y</c>-coordinate.
    </p>

    <p>
      We could store those values in separate variables:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
double x = 3.0;
double y = 4.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      However, the two values describe one conceptual object: a point.
      C++ provides <term>structures</term> so related values can be grouped
      into a single user-defined type.
    </p>
  </introduction>


  <subsection>
    <title>Defining a Structure</title>

    <p>
      A structure is defined using the <c>struct</c> keyword. The following
      definition creates a new type named <c>Point</c>:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The variables declared inside the structure are called
      <term>data members</term>. A <c>Point</c> has two data members:
      <c>x</c> and <c>y</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Notice the semicolon after the closing brace. A structure definition
      must end with a semicolon.
    </p>

    <p>
      Defining <c>Point</c> creates a new type. It does not yet create a
      particular point object.
    </p>

    <p>
      We can think of the structure definition as describing the layout of
      every <c>Point</c> object:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point
+----------------+
| x : double     |
| y : double     |
+----------------+
    ]]></program>
	
<p>
  A structure type must be defined before it is used to create objects or in
  function declarations. In the complete single-file programs in this book,
  structure definitions therefore appear near the top of the file, after the
  <c>#include</c> directives and before function declarations and
  <c>main</c>.
</p>
	
  </subsection>

<subsection xml:id="chapter5_creating-structure-objects">
    <title>Creating Structure Objects</title>

    <p>
      Once a structure type has been defined, we can create objects of that
      type in much the same way that we create variables of built-in types.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point location;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Here, <c>location</c> is an object whose type is <c>Point</c>.
      It contains its own <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> data members.
    </p>

    <p>
      Fundamental-type data members should not be left uninitialized.
      One safe way to create a structure object is to use empty braces:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point origin{};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      With this form, the numeric data members are value-initialized.
      For this <c>Point</c>, both <c>x</c> and <c>y</c> become
      <c>0.0</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
origin
+----------------+
| x : 0.0        |
| y : 0.0        |
+----------------+
    ]]></program>
	
	<p>
      Here is the complete program.
    </p>
	<program><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Point {
    double x;
    double y;
};

int main() {
    Point origin{};;

    return 0;
}
]]></program>
	
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Aggregate Initialization</title>

    <p>
      We can also provide initial values when an object is created.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point location{3.0, 4.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The values are assigned to the data members in the order in which
      the members appear in the structure definition.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
location
+----------------+
| x : 3.0        |
| y : 4.0        |
+----------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      This form is called <term>aggregate initialization</term>.
    </p>

    <p>
      For another structure:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      the statement:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      initializes <c>length</c> to <c>12.0</c> and <c>width</c> to
      <c>8.0</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room
+------------------+
| length : 12.0    |
| width  : 8.0     |
+------------------+
    ]]></program>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Accessing Data Members</title>

    <p>
      Use the dot operator, <c>.</c>, to access a data member of a structure
      object.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point location{3.0, 4.0};

std::cout << location.x << '\n';
std::cout << location.y << '\n';
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The expression <c>location.x</c> means the <c>x</c> data member
      belonging to the object named <c>location</c>.
    </p>

    <p>
      Similarly:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};

std::cout << room.length << '\n';
std::cout << room.width << '\n';
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Data members can also be changed using the dot operator:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room.length = 15.0;
room.width = 10.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      After these assignments, the state of <c>room</c> is:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
room
+------------------+
| length : 15.0    |
| width  : 10.0    |
+------------------+
    ]]></program>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Each Object Has Its Own Data</title>

    <p>
      Different objects of the same structure type have their own copies of
      the data members.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point first{2.0, 5.0};
Point second{8.0, 1.0};
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      These are two separate <c>Point</c> objects:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
first                     second
+--------------+          +--------------+
| x : 2.0      |          | x : 8.0      |
| y : 5.0      |          | y : 1.0      |
+--------------+          +--------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Changing <c>first.x</c> does not change <c>second.x</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
first.x = 10.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Now the objects contain:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
first                     second
+--------------+          +--------------+
| x : 10.0     |          | x : 8.0      |
| y : 5.0      |          | y : 1.0      |
+--------------+          +--------------+
    ]]></program>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Copying Structure Objects</title>

    <p>
      An object of a structure type can be copied to another object of the
      same type.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
Point first{3.0, 4.0};
Point second = first;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The values of <c>first</c>'s data members are copied into
      <c>second</c>.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
first                     second
+--------------+          +--------------+
| x : 3.0      |          | x : 3.0      |
| y : 4.0      |          | y : 4.0      |
+--------------+          +--------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      The objects are still independent. Changing one object afterward does
      not automatically change the other.
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
second.x = 9.0;
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      Now:
    </p>

    <program><![CDATA[
first                     second
+--------------+          +--------------+
| x : 3.0      |          | x : 9.0      |
| y : 4.0      |          | y : 4.0      |
+--------------+          +--------------+
    ]]></program>

    <p>
      This copying behavior will become important in the next section when
      we pass structure objects to functions.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>A Complete Example</title>

    <listing label="chapter5_structures_AC_1">
      <title>
        This program creates and uses two <c>Rectangle</c> objects.
      </title>

      <program interactive="activecode"
               line-numbers="yes"
               label="chapter5_structures_AC_1_editor">
        <code><![CDATA[
#include <iostream>

struct Rectangle {
    double length;
    double width;
};

int main() {
    Rectangle room{12.0, 8.0};
    Rectangle table{6.0, 3.0};

    std::cout << "Room length: " << room.length << '\n';
    std::cout << "Room width: " << room.width << '\n';

    std::cout << "Table length: " << table.length << '\n';
    std::cout << "Table width: " << table.width << '\n';

    return 0;
}
        ]]></code>
      </program>
    </listing>

    <p>
      Both <c>room</c> and <c>table</c> have type <c>Rectangle</c>, but each
      object stores its own <c>length</c> and <c>width</c>.
    </p>
  </subsection>


  <subsection>
    <title>Check Your Understanding</title>

    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_1">
      <statement>
        <p>
          In the following structure definition, which names are data members?
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
struct Temperature {
    double high;
    double low;
};
        ]]></program>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>Temperature</c> only</p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>Temperature</c> is the name of the structure type.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <p><c>high</c> and <c>low</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The variables declared inside the structure are its data members.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <p><c>double</c> and <c>Temperature</c></p>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>double</c> is a type, and <c>Temperature</c> is the
              user-defined structure type.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_2">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Given:
        </p>

        <program><![CDATA[
Point location{7.0, 2.0};
        ]]></program>

        <p>
          which expression accesses the <c>y</c> data member of
          <c>location</c>?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>Point.y</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Use the object name before the dot operator.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>location-y</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              Structure members are accessed using the dot operator.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>location.y</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The dot operator accesses a data member belonging to an object.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>y.location</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The object name appears before the dot, followed by the member
              name.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_3">
      <statement>
        <p>Consider:</p>

        <program><![CDATA[
Point first{3.0, 4.0};
Point second = first;

second.x = 10.0;
        ]]></program>

        <p>
          What is the value of <c>first.x</c> after these statements?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement><p><c>3.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>second</c> received a copy. Changing <c>second.x</c>
              does not change <c>first.x</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>4.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              <c>4.0</c> is the value of <c>first.y</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement><p><c>10.0</c></p></statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The assignment changes <c>second.x</c>, not
              <c>first.x</c>.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_4">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Which structure definition is written correctly?
        </p>
      </statement>

      <choices>
        <choice>
          <statement>
            <program><![CDATA[
struct Robot {
    int battery_level;
    double speed;
}
            ]]></program>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              A structure definition must end with a semicolon after the
              closing brace.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice correct="yes">
          <statement>
            <program><![CDATA[
struct Robot {
    int battery_level;
    double speed;
};
            ]]></program>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The data members are declared inside the braces, and the
              structure definition ends with a semicolon.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>

        <choice>
          <statement>
            <program><![CDATA[
struct Robot;
{
    int battery_level;
    double speed;
}
            ]]></program>
          </statement>
          <feedback>
            <p>
              The opening brace belongs immediately after the structure
              name in this definition.
            </p>
          </feedback>
        </choice>
      </choices>
    </exercise>


    <exercise label="chapter5_structures_5"
              number="yes"
              adaptive="yes"
              indentation="hide">
      <statement>
        <p>
          Arrange the blocks to define a <c>Robot</c> structure with an
          integer <c>battery_level</c> and a <c>double</c>
          <c>speed</c>.
        </p>
      </statement>

      <blocks>
        <block order="1">
          <cline>struct Robot {</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="2">
          <cline>    int battery_level;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="3">
          <cline>    double speed;</cline>
        </block>

        <block order="4">
          <cline>};</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>Robot {</cline>
        </block>

        <block correct="no">
          <cline>    speed double;</cline>
        </block>
      </blocks>
    </exercise>

  </subsection>

</section>
